WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.000
Cut a ganglion cell off from rods and cones.

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.460
If it still responds to light, whose response is it?

3
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:11.510
Here is the logic.

4
00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.310
Rods and cones reach ganglion cells through synapses.

5
00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:17.790
Block those synapses with drugs.

6
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.880
If a ganglion cell still responds once the block is verified, the response is most

7
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:24.880
likely its own.

8
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:29.923
In 2002, Berson, Dunn and Takao did this in rat retina.

9
00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:33.847
First they labelled ganglion cells from the SCN.

10
00:00:34.527 --> 00:00:36.767
That told them which cells project to the clock.

11
00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:41.607
Then they recorded from those labelled cells, while the blockers were applied.

12
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:47.697
Predict. Suppose the rod and cone relay were the cell's only light signal.

13
00:00:48.597 --> 00:00:50.417
What should the recording show after the block?

14
00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.130
There should be no light-evoked change.

15
00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:03.030
But one remained. The labelled cells still depolarized to light.

16
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.920
So the response was intrinsic, made within the ganglion cell itself.

17
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.540
They also recorded from a cell body removed from the retina.

18
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.620
That is a stronger test, with no synapses left at all.

19
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.210
So one cell can give two kinds of light response.

20
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.450
Put both on one time axis.

21
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:28.240
The relayed response, from rods and cones, usually starts fast and works in dim

22
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.320
light. It can have both brief and lasting parts.

23
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.680
The cell’s own response is usually slow to start and needs brighter light.

24
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.920
It is often long-lasting, though it adapts too.

25
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.140
When the light goes off, it decays slowly.

26
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.880
The cell stays depolarized for a while.

27
00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:50.823
In people, these cells drive the post-illumination pupil response.

28
00:01:51.783 --> 00:01:54.563
The pupil stays small for a while after light goes off.

29
00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:00.987
Why would slow and sustained be useful?

30
00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:04.387
The clock and the pupil need to know how bright it is.

31
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.420
A camera must follow fast changes.

32
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.020
A light meter needs a steady reading.

33
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:13.173
The cells that serve the clock and the pupil.

34
00:02:13.173 --> 00:02:15.233
are mostly a type called M1.

35
00:02:16.053 --> 00:02:18.873
They report how bright the world is, not what is in it.

36
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:25.293
The rat response fitted a pigment that peaks near 484 nanometres.

37
00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:28.713
That is a rat estimate, not a human one.

38
00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:34.227
In the same volume of the journal, Hattar and colleagues showed that these rat cells

39
00:02:34.227 --> 00:02:35.427
carried melanopsin.

40
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.730
And melanopsin-marked mouse axons reached the SCN.

41
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:44.613
A response, a pigment and a target now pointed to one set of cells.

42
00:02:45.533 --> 00:02:48.193
Their signature is slow, insensitive and sustained.
